; STRING LITERALS, END TO END ON FOUR TARGETS — docs/spec/string-literals.md.
;
; The six escapes are DERIVED rather than borrowed from a host: two because the
; delimiter and the escape introducer cannot denote themselves, three because
; the source grammar owns tab and the line terminators, and `\u{H…}` because
; totality demands that every scalar value be writable. This builds a string out
; of all six and requires the four hosts to produce the same bytes.
;
; prints: string
;
; ASCII ONLY, and the reason is a real per-target divergence rather than caution:
; `System.out` on the JVM encodes in the PLATFORM CHARSET, so a printed `é` is
; one byte on a Windows console and two on Go and V8 — a property of PRINTING,
; not of the literal, which was confirmed by asking the JVM for the string's
; UTF-16 length instead (3, so the literal is intact). Printing a string is not
; portable and strings.md §2 already says why; what this case can check is that
; the six escapes denote the same scalars everywhere.
;
; The non-ASCII half is covered where it can be: `emit/strlit_test.go` renders
; EVERY one of the 1,112,064 scalar values for both host string shapes, checks
; the output is ASCII, and decodes the UTF-16 form back to the scalar it came
; from. A table of examples cannot establish totality; that test can.
;
; `\u{41}` is `A`, `\u{7A}` is `z`. Written that way rather than as themselves so
; the general escape is exercised on a value every host must agree about.

; inputs: 0
; expect: A	"\z

(export run)

(def run (fn (n) "\u{41}\t\"\\\u{7A}"))
